!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/service.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/net.tn"
@doc:"""
  A vocabulary for describing a service: what it can be asked to do (exchanges, grouped into protocols)
  and how HTTP exposes that (an http projection of endpoints). A schema importing this one is a service
  description, and `TsonApiDescription` reads it.

  ONE ROOT NAME. Every entry of this layer, its annotations included, is a member of the schema map
  `service` nested in its root, and is named by its path from the root: `service.protocol`,
  `service.binding`, `@service.summary`. Every root name of an import closure is reserved in each importer
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), and a description imports this layer beside the domain schemas it describes,
  so a layer declaring `document` or `resource` at its root would forbid a domain those names. Nested in
  one map, it reserves `service` alone. An entry refers to another by the same path, from inside the map
  as from outside it, since a name resolves in the schema's one namespace.

  The layer describes a service from the protocol's side, not the host language's. A protocol is a named
  set of exchanges, each one request-and-reply: a request record, a response record and error records. An
  http projection is HTTP's own constructs -- verbs, paths, headers, statuses, media types -- bridged to an
  exchange's request record by placement. A language is reached from that side, through the join
  (`design/invoke.md`), and no parameter list, return type or exception is ever written here.

  One vocabulary, and two entries a description writes for what it serves. `service.protocol` and `service.http` are
  RECORDS, each holding one keyed field. A protocol's `exchanges` is a namespace ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1), a
  `service.exchanges` mapping exchange names to exchanges, since a binding's reference walks into it. A projection's
  `resources` is a plain map, a `service.resources` mapping path templates to resources, since nothing references an
  endpoint. An entry applying either is a value, never a type. Everything inside them -- `service.exchange`,
  `service.resource`, `service.endpoint`, `service.operation`, `service.binding`, `service.document`,
  `service.exemption` -- is an ordinary record too. A record composes (`service.operation => service.endpoint &
  service.signature`) and is admitted as a subtype where its base is expected ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.2). Beside them a
  description may declare the security schemes its projections and endpoints name, each a value entry tagged with a
  subtype of `service.security_scheme`: `bearer => !service.http_auth { scheme: Bearer }`.

    orders => !service.protocol {
      exchanges: {
        @service.safe get_order => { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
        place_order             => { request: new_order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found] }
      }
    }

    orders_http => !service.http {
      binds: [orders]
      resources: {
        "/orders"      => { POST => !service.binding { exchange: orders.exchanges.place_order  status: 201 } }
        "/orders/{id}" => { GET  => !service.binding { exchange: orders.exchanges.get_order } }
      }
    }

  Exchanges are keys inside their protocol's namespace, and endpoints are values two maps down -- keyed by
  path, then by verb -- so neither is a global entry: two protocols may both declare `get_order`, and an
  http projection needs no invented operation names. An exchange's qualified name is its protocol's entry,
  the field `exchanges`, then its key: `orders.exchanges.get_order`.

  What an http projection claims about the protocols behind it is written in FIELDS beside its resources:
  `binds` names the protocols, and `not_bound` the exchanges it claims and does not bind. A protocol lists
  its exchanges and nothing more; there is no inheritance between protocols, and a projection binding two
  names both.

  Three ways to use it:
    protocol only     a `!service.protocol`; nothing HTTP in the document.
    web service only  a `!service.http` of `!service.operation`s, each carrying its signature INLINE.
    both              a `!service.protocol`, and a `!service.http` whose `binds` names it, with
                      `!service.binding`s naming its exchanges.
  An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value -- a schema document served as bytes, a page -- is a
  `!service.document`, listing its media types where the others state a response type.

  THE POSITIONAL FORM, AND WHAT IT COSTS. `service.resource`, `service.protocol` and `service.http` each
  have exactly one bare REQUIRED field, so [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.6 lets the map stand in for it:
  `"/orders" => { GET => ... }` is `"/orders" => { endpoints: { GET => ... } }`, and the tag is optional under
  the path key. The examples use it for a resource, and write a protocol and a projection in full, since a
  reference into a protocol spells `exchanges` whether the document wrote it or not, and a projection writing
  `binds` cannot use the form at all. The cost is a versioning one and it is the reason this is written down: adding a SECOND
  required field to any of the three withdraws the positional form from it, and every document written in
  it stops parsing. So a new field there is optional or it is a new type.

  WHAT THE RESOLVER CHECKS, AND WHAT THE READER DOES. A relation that names something is a reference the
  resolver judges at schema load. A binding's or an exemption's `exchange` is a `meta.ref<service.exchange>`,
  a qualified name walking an entry, its `exchanges` field and a key -- `orders.exchanges.place_order` --
  each name in `binds` a `meta.ref<service.protocol>`, and each name in a `security` a
  `meta.ref<service.security_scheme>`. So a name nothing declares, one naming the wrong kind of thing, or an
  exchange key the protocol does not hold is refused there, and the qualified name says which protocol it
  means. What relates two things beyond naming one is the reader's, when `TsonApiDescription` reads a
  description, and an unsound description has no model: a binding's or an exemption's exchange belonging to
  a protocol the projection binds, the `binds` claim, the placement of a request record over a path, a verb
  or a status against what the exchange declares, an error type fixing its status, a media type the server
  can speak, no two endpoints sharing a verb and a path, and a response header's template expanding from a
  scalar field of the response type.
  """
!schema {
  service => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      The transport-neutral contract of one request-and-reply. A plain record on purpose: it is what
      `exchange` and an inline `operation` share.

      `request` absent means the exchange takes nothing; `response` absent means it returns nothing. An http
      endpoint over a signature with no response writes `status: 204`, `205` or `304`, a status that carries no
      body, and `TsonApiDescription` refuses one that does not: `service.endpoint`'s default 200 is for an endpoint
      with a response. `errors` lists BUSINESS errors -- the request was schema-valid and the domain said no -- each
      a type composing `problem.details`. A transport's own refusals (a body failing its schema, an Accept it
      cannot satisfy) are not listed: every exchange has them and the error boundary answers them.
      """
    signature => {
      request?:         meta.type_ref
      response?:        meta.type_ref
      errors?:          [meta.type_ref]
    }

    @doc:"""
      An exchange: one request-and-reply, a signature and nothing more. Its NAME is the key it sits under in a
      protocol. A record, not a constructor: it is only ever a map value. The empty trailing body is what
      makes it a type of its own -- `exchange => service.signature` would be an alias, and an alias is a hop to its
      target, so the values would read back as signatures.
      """
    exchange => service.signature & {}

    @doc:"""
      A protocol's exchanges, keyed by name. A namespace type, so a qualified name enters it: a
      `meta.ref<service.exchange>` reaches an exchange as `orders.exchanges.place_order`, the protocol's entry,
      this field and then the key. An exchange is a map VALUE under its name, so its name is scoped to the
      protocol, and annotations written before the key -- `@doc`, `@service.summary`, `@service.safe`,
      `@service.idempotent`, `@service.destructive` -- document it and state its facts.
      """
    exchanges => !meta.namespace { key_type: service.exchange_name  value_type: service.exchange }

    @doc:"""
      A protocol: a named set of exchanges, and nothing else. A record whose one field is the namespace of its
      exchanges, so a fact about the protocol as a whole is a field beside it, where a value of a namespace type
      is the map alone. Its one required field is bare, so the positional form admits the map written alone,
      and a second required field would withdraw it.
      """
    protocol => {
      exchanges: service.exchanges
    }

    @doc:"RFC 9110's request methods (§9), called verbs so that `method` names nothing in this layer."
    http_verb => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

    @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
    status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

    @doc:"""
      The naming roles of the namespaces this layer borrows -- one per namespace, as the kernel declares
      `meta.type_name` and `meta.param_name` over `meta.identifier`. A key typed by a role
      enforces that namespace's grammar and names the namespace in the refusal ("'exchange_name': 'place
      order': U+0020 cannot appear in an identifier"), where `type_name` would misname the namespace doing the
      refusing. A namespace type's key is a name, so its role is an identifier family: where the authority's
      grammar is wider than TSON's identifier, the role is a `unicode.identifier_type` widened with `start_add`
      and `continue_add`. A plain map's key is no name, and its role may be a text family carrying a `pattern`,
      as `header_name` and `path_template` are; a `pattern` is I-Regexp, matched against the whole token, so no
      anchors.

      An exchange's name: an identifier in its protocol's namespace, not a type name.
      """
    exchange_name => meta.identifier

    @doc:"""
      An http projection's key: an absolute path written as a URI template (RFC 6570) at levels 1 and 2, whose
      variables name fields of the endpoint's request record. `{id}` expands a value percent-encoded;
      `{+schemaPath}`, reserved expansion, leaves its `/` unencoded, which is what an identity path with
      slashes in it needs. The fragment operator is not admitted, since a server never receives a fragment,
      and nor are levels 3 and 4 -- several variables to an expression, query forms, prefixes and explode --
      which describe expansion rather than a route a server can match. A variable name is RFC 6570's varname,
      ASCII letters, digits and `_`, so a request field it binds is spelt that way: a field named `order-id`
      cannot be bound from the path, and travels in the query, a header or the body.

      A text family with a `pattern`, as `header_name` is, and no name. A path is where the schema stops and HTTP
      begins: a server matches it against a request's target, and nothing in a description references it, so it
      takes none of a name's grammar or hygiene. The `pattern` is ASCII-exact: a `/`, then any run of RFC 3986's
      path characters -- unreserved, sub-delimiters, `:`, `@` and `/` -- percent-encoded octets `%XX`, and
      expressions `{var}` or `{+var}`. Nothing else is admitted, a bare `%`, `{` or `}` included, so percent-encoding
      is the one way a path carries any other character. A key is held as written, `unicode.text_type`'s
      normalization being `NONE` unless stated.
      """
    path_template => !unicode.text_type {
      pattern: "/([A-Za-z0-9._~-]|[!$&'()*+,;=]|[:@/]|%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}|\\{[+]?[A-Za-z0-9_]+\\})*"
    }

    @doc:"""
      A header field name: an RFC 9110 token, compared without case as RFC 9110 §5.1 has it -- held folded under
      `ASCII_CASEFOLD`, so `Idempotency-Key` is `idempotency-key`, the form HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 put on the wire.
      [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5 judges the `pattern` against the value put into the type's form, and `ASCII_CASEFOLD`, the
      comparison HTTP field names state for themselves, maps `A` to `Z` and nothing else, so a name spelt with a
      fullwidth letter (U+FF23 for `C`) or a long s (U+017F) stays outside the token grammar and is refused, where
      `NFKC_CASEFOLD` would fold either into ASCII and admit it. It keys a map field rather than a namespace, so
      it is a text family with a `pattern`.
      """
    header_name => !unicode.text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+"  normalization: ASCII_CASEFOLD }

    @doc:"""
      Where a request record's fields go: the bridge from HTTP's constructs to the exchange's request record,
      and the place where a protocol and the http projection over it are allowed to look different. The verb
      and the path are not here: they are the keys an operation sits under, in its resource and its
      projection.

      An exchange has one request record, and the endpoint distributes that record's fields across the HTTP
      request: a `{segment}` in the resource's path binds the field of that name; `query` names fields
      carried as query parameters; `headers` maps a field to the header that carries it; `body` names ONE
      field carried as the whole body, unwrapped. Whatever is left is the remainder, and the remainder is
      the body -- unless the verb is GET or HEAD, or `body` already named a field, in which case it is the
      query. Every field lands in exactly one place, and a field carried in the path, the query or a
      header must be a scalar -- a URL segment cannot carry a record. That is what makes the mapping
      checkable: a field placed twice, a name the request does not declare, or a container in the path is
      a bad endpoint rather than a surprise.

      There is no parameter list. A parameter-only endpoint declares a one-field request record
      (`schema_ref => { schemaPath: text }`) and binds it from the path -- an exchange that takes anything
      takes exactly ONE record, which is what lets one rule serve every endpoint and the check see every
      field. `request` on `service.signature` is optional because an exchange may take nothing at all, and such an
      endpoint admits no placement: a `{segment}` on it is refused, having no field to bind. A request that is
      present and is not a record is refused too, since there would be nothing to distribute.
      """
    placement => {
      query?:   [meta.identifier]
      headers?: {meta.identifier => service.header_name}
      body?:    meta.identifier
    }

    @doc:"""
      What sits under a verb, under a path, in an http projection: the base record of `operation`, `binding`
      and `document`, carrying what each has -- the placement of a request record, the status of the response,
      and the media types the endpoint speaks. It is `abstract` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2): it has no values of its
      own, so a value at an `endpoint`-typed slot is tagged with the subtype it is and admitted as that subtype
      (§7.2), and an untagged value, or one tagged `!service.endpoint`, is refused by the resolver naming the
      subtypes, in every mode. The sealed interface `Endpoint` the reader binds it to is the Java side of the same
      fact. The tag is the whole difference between the one-declaration form and the two-declaration form, and it
      is read before anything else.

      `status` is the status of the success response; there is no list of responses, because an exchange has
      one response type. The default 200 is for an endpoint with a response: one whose signature has no response
      type writes `status: 204` (or 205 or 304, a status that carries no body), and `TsonApiDescription` refuses
      an operation or a binding that does not. An error's status is the `status` member its own type pins --
      `sku_not_found => problem.details & { status: = 404  sku: text }` -- so an error type an endpoint names MUST fix
      it, and `TsonApiDescription` refuses one that does not. A transport's own refusals -- a body failing its
      schema, an Accept nothing satisfies -- are not listed: every endpoint has them, and the error boundary
      answers each with a `problem.details`.

      Every endpoint but a `document` speaks `application/tson`; `media_types` (net.tn's `media_type`) lists
      what it speaks beside it, absent meaning `application/tson` alone. One set serves both directions,
      request body and response; a split between what an endpoint accepts and what it produces is a later
      field, not this one. A label is listed, never implied: an endpoint answering JSON lists
      `application/json`, `application/tson+json` or both, and Accept chooses among what is listed. Media
      types, because the projection takes its vocabulary from HTTP's own registries (RFC 9110 §8.3.1), so a
      new encoding is a new registered name and never an edit to this layer. `TsonApiDescription` refuses at
      startup a media type no codec it serves can speak, so a description naming an encoding the server lacks
      fails to start and never answers 500.

      Documentation is annotation, not fields: `@doc` before the verb key is the long form and `@service.summary` the
      short one, both surviving into the read value as the key's annotations, since an endpoint is a map value
      and a map key carries annotations. A binding's exchange carries its own on its key in the protocol.

      `security` names the security schemes the endpoint accepts, as alternatives -- a credential for any one of
      them will do -- each a reference to a value entry tagged with a subtype of `service.security_scheme`,
      which the resolver judges at load. Present, it REPLACES the projection's `security` for this endpoint;
      `security: []` written out says the endpoint is open; absent, the endpoint inherits the projection's; and
      with both absent it is open. The description states a scheme and the reader reads it: nothing here
      enforces one.

      `response_headers` maps a header the success response carries to its value, an RFC 6570 level-1 template
      over the fields of the endpoint's RESPONSE type -- `Location => "/orders/{id}"` -- or a literal when it
      holds no variable -- `Cache-Control => "max-age=3600"`. A binding's response type is its exchange's. A
      variable names a field of that record which is a scalar, and `TsonApiDescription` refuses one that does
      not at startup, as it refuses a request field placed where it cannot travel; a `document`, or any
      endpoint with no response type, admits a literal only. The header names are `header_name`s, held folded.

      Deliberately absent, each a decision: links, callbacks, examples, tags, servers, and more than one success
      response. Adding one is a later version under [TSON-SCHEMA] §3.5 once this is published, never an edit --
      and a new kind of endpoint is a new record composing this one, admitted wherever an `endpoint` is.
      """
    endpoint => abstract service.placement & {
      status?:            service.status_code ~ 200
      media_types?:       [media_type]
      security?:          [meta.ref<service.security_scheme>]
      response_headers?:  {service.header_name => text}
    }

    @doc:"""
      An endpoint whose body is not a TSON value: a schema document served as the bytes it was published as, a
      page. Its `media_types` are the whole contract of its body -- usually one, `text/html` or the media type a
      schema document was published as -- and `TsonApiDescription` refuses a document listing none.
      `application/tson` is not implied for a document as it is for the other endpoint kinds. Its success body
      is not judged as a value, only labelled. It may still take a request record to place and name business
      errors, and it restates `request?` and `errors?` rather than composing `service.signature` because the
      signature carries `response?` too, and a document has no response type to judge.
      """
    document => service.endpoint & {
      request?:   meta.type_ref
      errors?:    [meta.type_ref]
    }

    @doc:"""
      The short form of an endpoint's or an exchange's documentation, written before its key --
      `@service.summary:"Place an order." POST => !service.binding { ... }`. Declared as the kernel declares `doc`;
      `@doc` remains the long form. Not a field, because documentation is metadata about a declaration rather
      than part of what it declares, and an annotation type declared here reaches every schema importing
      this one.
      """
    summary => @meta.annotation text

    @doc:"""
      The exchange has no effect a caller can observe: calling it is a read. `safe` is the first of three facts
      about an exchange that a caller composing calls needs -- an agent planning a batch, deciding what may be
      retried, reordered or must be confirmed first -- and has no HTTP verb to read them from; `idempotent` and
      `destructive`, declared after it, are the other two. Bare markers before the exchange's key:
      `@service.safe get_order => { ... }`, `@service.idempotent cancel_order => { ... }`. `@service.safe` implies
      `@service.idempotent`. Annotations rather than fields because they describe the exchange, not its request or
      response; the http projection CHECKS its verb against them (GET must be safe; PUT and DELETE idempotent) and
      never derives one from the other. `safe` and `idempotent` are RFC 9110's words for the same facts.

      `@service.destructive` is the third: the exchange has an effect a caller cannot undo, and a caller should
      confirm before invoking it -- `@service.idempotent @service.destructive cancel_order => { ... }`. It is the one
      fact an LLM tool-calling convention asks for that the other two do not state. `@service.safe` and
      `@service.destructive` exclude each other, since a safe exchange has no effect to undo, and the reader refuses an
      exchange marked both. Nothing derives it from a verb and no verb implies it: a `DELETE` may cancel a reservation
      that can be made again, and a `POST` may wipe a table. The http projection refuses a destructive exchange bound
      at GET or HEAD, as it refuses an unsafe one there.
      """
    safe        => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"The exchange may be repeated with the effect of one call, so a caller may retry it; `@service.safe` implies it."
    idempotent  => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"The exchange has an effect a caller cannot undo, so a caller confirms it first; it excludes `@service.safe`."
    destructive => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"An endpoint with its signature INLINE -- the one-declaration form, for a projection with no protocol behind it."
    operation => service.endpoint & service.signature

    @doc:"""
      An endpoint whose signature is an EXCHANGE's -- the two-declaration form. `exchange` is a reference the
      resolver judges, its qualified name saying which protocol declares it (`orders.exchanges.get_order`, the
      protocol's entry, its `exchanges` field and the exchange's key), so no binding is ambiguous between two
      protocols declaring one name. That the protocol is one the projection binds is the reader's check.
      """
    binding => service.endpoint & {
      exchange: meta.ref<service.exchange>
    }

    @doc:"""
      One path: its endpoints, keyed by verb. A record with one bare required field, so the map stands in
      for it and the tag is optional under the path key -- `"/orders" => { POST => !service.binding { ... } }` and
      `"/orders" => !service.resource { endpoints: { ... } }` read alike.
      """
    resource => {
      endpoints: {service.http_verb => service.endpoint}
    }

    @doc:"""
      Why a claimed exchange has no endpoint here: an exchange documented ahead of being built, or served by
      another process behind the same proxy. `reason` is required, because an exemption somebody has to justify
      in a greppable string is a decision where a boolean is a hole. `exchange` names exactly the exchange it
      covers, as a binding's does -- `orders_v2.exchanges.refund` -- so it can never quietly cover a second
      protocol's exchange of the same name. The resolver judges the reference; that it names an exchange of a
      protocol the projection binds is the reader's check. A list of records rather than a map keyed by
      exchange: a map key is a value of the map's own key family, never a reference.
      """
    exemption => {
      exchange: meta.ref<service.exchange>
      reason:   text
    }

    @doc:"""
      An http projection's resources keyed by path template, each holding endpoints keyed by verb -- the shape
      OpenAPI's `paths` has, arrived at from the key types rather than copied. A plain map, not a namespace: a
      path key is the URL's own grammar -- a `path_template` -- which is why an endpoint needs no invented name and
      why `/orders/{id}` needs no identifier minted from it. A qualified name walks a namespace's keys and no
      other, so none walks a path key, and nothing references a resource or an endpoint.
      """
    resources => {service.path_template => service.resource}

    @doc:"""
      An http projection: its resources, and what it claims about the protocols behind it. Named for the
      transport it projects a protocol onto, as a gRPC projection of the same protocol would be: it is one
      projection among several, not the description of the service.

      `binds` names the protocols it binds, and is a CLAIM the reader holds it to: every exchange of every
      protocol named has a `binding` naming it, or an `exemption` in `not_bound` giving the reason it does not.
      The word is the one `!service.binding` and `not_bound` use, since binding an exchange to HTTP is what the
      projection does with it. The claim is held per (protocol, exchange), not per name: two bound protocols may
      both declare `get_order`, and binding one of them says nothing about the other. A projection with no
      `binds` is a web service on its own terms, its endpoints `!service.operation`s. Each name and each
      exemption's exchange is a reference the resolver judges at schema load.

      `security` is the projection's default: the security schemes every endpoint accepts, as alternatives,
      unless the endpoint writes its own `security`, which replaces it. Each name is a reference to a value entry
      tagged with a subtype of `service.security_scheme`, judged at load.

      Its one required field, `resources`, is a map and bare, so a projection writing nothing else may write the
      map alone; one writing `binds` writes `resources:` too, since a brace holds fields or keys, never both.
      """
    http => {
      binds?:     [meta.ref<service.protocol>]
      not_bound?: [service.exemption]
      security?:  [meta.ref<service.security_scheme>]
      resources:  service.resources
    }

    @doc:"""
      How a client proves who it is to an endpoint: the base record of `http_auth` and `api_key`. A description
      declares a scheme as a VALUE ENTRY at its root, tagged with the subtype it is --
      `bearer => !service.http_auth { scheme: Bearer }` -- and an http projection or an endpoint names it in
      `security`, one segment, a reference the resolver judges. It is `abstract` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2), so an
      untagged scheme, or one tagged `!service.security_scheme`, is refused by the resolver naming the subtypes, in
      every mode, as an untagged `endpoint` is; the sealed interface `SecurityScheme` it binds to is the Java side
      of the same fact. It declares no field: what a scheme says is its subtype's.
      """
    security_scheme => abstract { }

    @doc:"""
      An HTTP authentication scheme (RFC 9110 §11): the client sends `Authorization: <scheme> <credentials>`.
      `scheme` is the scheme's registered name -- `Bearer` (RFC 6750), `Basic` (RFC 7617) -- held folded.
      """
    http_auth => service.security_scheme & { scheme: service.auth_scheme }

    @doc:"""
      A key the client carries in a header or a query parameter: `in` says which, and `name` is the header's or
      the parameter's name. `name` is `text` rather than a `header_name` because a query parameter's name is no
      header name; when `in` is `header` the reader holds it as one, folded under `ASCII_CASEFOLD` and refused
      unless it is an RFC 9110 token, so `X-Api-Key` is `x-api-key`.
      """
    api_key => service.security_scheme & { in: service.api_key_in  name: text }

    @doc:"""
      An authentication scheme's name: an RFC 9110 token, compared without case as RFC 9110 §11.1 has it -- held
      folded under `ASCII_CASEFOLD`, so `Bearer` is `bearer`, and a name a compatibility fold would bring into
      ASCII is refused, as a `header_name`'s is. A text family with a `pattern`, as `header_name` is.
      """
    auth_scheme => !unicode.text_type { pattern: "[!#$%&'*+.^_`|~0-9A-Za-z-]+"  normalization: ASCII_CASEFOLD }

    @doc:"Where an `api_key` travels: a request header or a query parameter."
    api_key_in => !enum [header query]
  }
}
